The mechanism is explicit, but an observable difference occurs only when runtime tree data contains overloaded objects and active filter patterns produce different results for their ordinary and overloaded names; the supplied diff does not establish that those conditions occur.

Database navigator filtering now treats an overloaded object's ordinary name and overloaded name as candidate identities. The filter API accepts multiple names and considers a pattern matched when it matches any supplied name, affecting both inclusion and exclusion rules.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When loading database navigator children, an item implementing DBPOverloadedObject is now filtered using both its ordinary object name and its overloaded name. Consequently, such an item can be shown because either name satisfies an inclusion pattern, or hidden because either name satisfies an exclusion pattern.
  • Callers of DBSObjectFilter.matches can now submit multiple candidate names. For each configured pattern, matching any one candidate counts as a match; existing one-name calls retain the same basic evaluation path.

